{"BlockedEmail.should_block? updates match_count via read-modify-write and calls record.save without checking success, so concurrent attempts can lose increments and failures are silently ignored. This makes match_count/last_match_at unreliable and adds unnecessary write load on every match.": 0.8784777522087097, "In EmailValidator#email_in_restriction_setting?, the regex @(#{domains}) lacks an end-of-string anchor. For whitelist mode, user@company.com.evil.org falsely matches company.com and passes validation; for blacklist mode, user@mailinator.com.legit.org is incorrectly blocked.": 0.7634496688842773, "BlockedEmail.should_block? matches emails with exact string equality, so users can bypass blocking by changing email casing (e.g., BLOCK@x vs block@x). The client-side rejectedEmails cache is also case-sensitive, so the UI will not consistently prevent resubmission with different casing.": 0.956529974937439, "EmailValidator emits I18n.t('user.email.blocked') but only server.en.yml defines this key, so non-English server locales will show 'translation missing' strings. The client also has no user.email.blocked translation, so the UI cannot present a blocked-specific inline reason.": 0.603449285030365, "BlockedEmail.should_block? writes to the database (incrementing match_count) during model validation. In users_controller.rb#create, user.valid? and user.save both trigger the full validation chain, so should_block? is called twice per blocked registration attempt, double-counting every match.": 0.905943751335144, "The frontend indiscriminately adds any email that receives backend errors to rejectedEmails. If an email fails for non-block reasons (e.g., 'already taken'), it will be permanently rejected on the client side with a generic 'invalid' message.": 0.8930791616439819}